WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:04.700
A supplier sends a SOC 2 report,
and someone files it as audited.

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:07.855
Four pages decide whether
that means anything.

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:11.013
First, the type and the period.

00:00:11.328 --> 00:00:13.458
A Type 1 report covers one day.

00:00:13.698 --> 00:00:17.408
A Type 2 report covers a period,
and it ended months ago.

00:00:17.673 --> 00:00:21.563
The standard says a Type 2 ordinarily
covers at least six months.

00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:24.093
The report is a photograph of the past,

00:00:24.503 --> 00:00:26.153
not a guarantee for next year.

00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:28.207
Second, the scope.

00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:32.392
The opinion covers exactly what
the system description says.

00:00:32.682 --> 00:00:35.372
Your module or region may not be in it.

00:00:35.597 --> 00:00:38.887
A cloud provider may be carved out,
and so not tested.

00:00:39.197 --> 00:00:42.147
A clean opinion on the
wrong scope is no assurance.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.420
Third, your part.

00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:48.465
Every report lists the controls
the supplier assumed you run.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.420
The auditor did not test them.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.580
Typical lines:

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.440
who gets access, who removes leavers,
who reviews the logs.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.290
They rarely reach the
people who run our side.

00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:01.233
Fourth, the exceptions at the back.

00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:03.303
The opinion is on page three.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:05.383
The exceptions are on page sixty.

00:01:05.768 --> 00:01:08.218
A clean opinion with exceptions is normal.

00:01:08.548 --> 00:01:10.918
Read each one for what
it means for our service.

00:01:12.178 --> 00:01:15.128
And configuration,
access and data use are never tested.

00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:17.393
What good looks like.

00:01:17.678 --> 00:01:22.068
Type, period, date and auditor are
recorded before anyone reads on.

00:01:22.368 --> 00:01:24.518
A scope map has one line per service:

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:26.998
in, carved out, or not covered.

00:01:27.303 --> 00:01:29.873
Every assumed control gets a named owner.

00:01:30.198 --> 00:01:32.948
Every exception gets a
rating for our service.

00:01:33.288 --> 00:01:35.358
This takes an afternoon per report.

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:40.258
NIS2 requires supply chain
security measures, in Article 21,

00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:43.038
and the report is one way to show them.

00:01:44.797 --> 00:01:46.007
A suggested first step.

00:01:46.292 --> 00:01:49.942
Is our service in scope,
in a Type 2, less than a year old?

00:01:50.157 --> 00:01:52.407
Who owns our list of assumed controls?

00:01:52.832 --> 00:01:57.582
Accepting a weaker report can be
reasonable, as long as it is a decision.

00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:00.027
Four pages.

00:02:00.437 --> 00:02:03.727
Read the report for what it covers,
and what it leaves to us.

